From: Fold 3 mainline port
Subject: [PATCH] media: qcom: camss: park clocks at XO before collapsing the
 power domain

camss raises its clock rates on the way up and has never lowered them again,
so a power domain is always collapsed with its RCGs still programmed for the
last streaming rate. On SM8350 that permanently wedges the IFE GDSC: the
power-down reports success, but every later power-up times out with PWR_ON
never asserting, the domain's virtual device drops into runtime-PM error, and
from then on every stream start fails with -EINVAL out of device_link_add() in
vfe_pm_domain_on(). Only a reboot recovers it, so in practice the camera can
be used exactly once per boot -- and because libcamera only ever opens VFE0,
that is one libcamera session per boot.

It was isolated by bisecting a camss session against the raw hardware through
/dev/mem, with camss out of the loop:

  - the GDSC alone cycles off/on indefinitely (50-70us per power-up)
  - so does it with all six IFE clock branches ungated and regated
  - so does it after a VFE global reset, after a WM_CGC_OVERRIDE write, after
    running the CSID RDI path, and after enabling the VFE write master
  - a full camss session wedges it even with all CSID and VFE programming
    skipped, and even streaming from the CSID's own test generator with no
    sensor, no CSIPHY and zero frames captured
  - a full camss session with clk_set_rate() skipped does NOT wedge it:
    four power cycles, GDSC healthy, and it still cycles by hand afterwards

which leaves the rate setting itself. Restoring the lowest rate in the
resource table is not enough: for the SM8350 IFE clocks that is already
338 MHz off a PLL and wedges the GDSC just as 720 MHz does. The state that
demonstrably survives a collapse is the one the clocks boot in, 19.2 MHz
straight off the crystal, which is exactly what a session with clk_set_rate()
skipped leaves behind.

So park every clock that has a rate table at XO before gating it and dropping
the domain. Clocks whose table is { 0 } are left alone.

Tested on a Galaxy Z Fold 3 (SM-F926B, SM8350) with the rear ultrawide
IMX258: 18 consecutive full-resolution raw captures in one boot where the
first had previously been the only one possible, two libcamera sessions in
one boot, and zero "stuck at" or "Failed to power up" messages.

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss.c b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss.c
index 2123f6388..ae9e882b8 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss.c
@@ -4585,6 +4912,45 @@ int camss_enable_clocks(int nclocks, struct camss_clock *clock,
  * @nclocks: Number of clocks in clock array
  * @clock: Clock array
  */
+/*
+ * camss_restore_clock_rates - Put clocks back to their lowest rate
+ * @nclocks: Number of clocks in the array
+ * @clock: Clock array
+ *
+ * Called before the clocks are gated and the power domain collapsed.
+ *
+ * camss raises these rates on the way up and has never lowered them again, so
+ * a domain is always collapsed with its RCGs still programmed for the last
+ * streaming rate. On SM8350 that permanently wedges the IFE GDSC: the
+ * power-down looks clean, but every later power-up times out with PWR_ON
+ * never asserting and only a reboot recovers it.
+ *
+ * That was isolated by bisecting a camss session against the raw hardware:
+ * driving the GDSC and the IFE clocks straight through /dev/mem cycles
+ * indefinitely, and so does a full camss session with clk_set_rate() skipped
+ * (four cycles, GDSC healthy), while a session that sets the rates wedges it
+ * on the second power-up -- even with all CSID and VFE programming skipped.
+ *
+ * Restore XO rather than the lowest entry in the resource table: for the
+ * SM8350 IFE clocks that lowest entry is already 338 MHz off a PLL, and
+ * leaving them there wedges the GDSC just as 720 MHz does. The state that
+ * demonstrably survives a collapse is the one the clocks boot in, 19.2 MHz
+ * straight off the crystal, which is what a session with clk_set_rate()
+ * skipped entirely leaves behind.
+ *
+ * Entries of { 0 } mean "leave this clock alone" and are skipped.
+ */
+#define CAMSS_CLOCK_PARK_RATE	19200000
+
+void camss_restore_clock_rates(int nclocks, struct camss_clock *clock)
+{
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < nclocks; i++)
+		if (clock[i].nfreqs && clock[i].freq[0])
+			clk_set_rate(clock[i].clk, CAMSS_CLOCK_PARK_RATE);
+}
+
 void camss_disable_clocks(int nclocks, struct camss_clock *clock)
 {
 	int i;
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss.h b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss.h
index 93d691c8a..442a1ab37 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss.h
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss.h
@@ -166,6 +167,7 @@ struct parent_dev_ops {
 void camss_add_clock_margin(u64 *rate);
 int camss_enable_clocks(int nclocks, struct camss_clock *clock,
 			struct device *dev);
+void camss_restore_clock_rates(int nclocks, struct camss_clock *clock);
 void camss_disable_clocks(int nclocks, struct camss_clock *clock);
 struct media_pad *camss_find_sensor_pad(struct media_entity *entity);
 s64 camss_get_link_freq(struct media_entity *entity, unsigned int bpp,
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-vfe.c b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-vfe.c
index 319d19158..b2b87fbbd 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-vfe.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-vfe.c
@@ -1156,6 +1157,7 @@ void vfe_put(struct vfe_device *vfe)
 			vfe->was_streaming = 0;
 			vfe->res->hw_ops->vfe_halt(vfe);
 		}
+		camss_restore_clock_rates(vfe->nclocks, vfe->clock);
 		camss_disable_clocks(vfe->nclocks, vfe->clock);
 		pm_runtime_put_sync(vfe->camss->dev);
 		vfe->res->hw_ops->pm_domain_off(vfe);
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid.c b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid.c
index 48459b46a..92d073c53 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid.c
@@ -738,6 +738,7 @@ static int csid_set_power(struct v4l2_subdev *sd, int on)
 		csid->res->hw_ops->hw_version(csid);
 	} else {
 		disable_irq(csid->irq);
+		camss_restore_clock_rates(csid->nclocks, csid->clock);
 		camss_disable_clocks(csid->nclocks, csid->clock);
 		regulator_bulk_disable(csid->num_supplies,
 				       csid->supplies);
